            <aside aria-labelledby="wheel-lock-take" class="pick-assessment">
              <h3 id="wheel-lock-take">Jerry&#x2019;s take</h3>
              <p>There is a lot of discussion in the RV community about how to protect your RV from being stolen. Some
                solutions cost hundreds of dollars.
                Where they all work to some extent, if someone wants to steal your RV bad enough, they'll do it. My
                philosophy falls in line with the story of
                the two people running from the lion saying we'll never outrun the lion. One turns to the other and
                says, "I don't have to outrun the lion... I just
                have to outrun you!". In my opinion the way to give the highest probability that your RV won't be stolen
                is to make it more difficult to steal than
                the RV sitting next to you. I use two of these claws, always both on the same side of the RV. The easiest way
                to disable them is to remove the whole tire.
                But if there is a clamp on each tire on one side and both tires have to be removed,
                your RV is not going to be rolling anywhere.</p><p> Easy to
                install on the tires and easy to remove.
                I do wish they had keyed both claws in the set with the same key. But you can't having everything. Still
                a good product.
              </p>
            </aside>

